WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.480
INTRO:
We all know the saying “All roads lead to Mr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:08.480
Wonderful.” While this isn’t always true, Kevin&nbsp;
is oftentimes the only shark to make an offer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.800
Now, his offers aren’t the cheapest given&nbsp;
that they’re riddled with royalties and debt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:24.720
but he usually asks for less equity than all the&nbsp;
other sharks. In many cases, he even offers lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:42.960
equity deals just to squeeze the other sharks and&nbsp;
force them to make better offers themselves. Kevin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:48.480
also never puts time limits on his deals like some&nbsp;
other Sharks. So, though Kevin is often portrayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.200
as the evil bald dude in the middle, I think&nbsp;
Kevin is actually the fairest shark of them all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.640
In most cases, he just wants a fair valuation&nbsp;
and a clear path to profitability for himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.520
and the entrepreneur. Despite this, Kevin&nbsp;
is often the last choice for entrepreneurs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:07.520
which is a shame given that Kevin is usually&nbsp;
the second wealthiest shark in the tank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:12.960
and the most straightforward. So, here’s the story&nbsp;
of Kevin O’Leary and how he became Mr. Wonderful.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.440
KEVIN O’LEARY:&nbsp;

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:22.960
Taking a look back, Kevin’s story dates back&nbsp;
to July 9, 1954, in Montreal, Canada. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:28.080
official name is actually Terence Thomas Kevin&nbsp;
O’Leary, so Kevin is not actually his first name.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.800
Anyway, Kevin’s father was a salesman while&nbsp;
his mother ran a small local business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:37.840
This provided the family a decent life for a&nbsp;
couple of years, but this didn’t last long.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:43.360
You see, Kevin’s father slowly became more&nbsp;
and more of an alcoholic which caused several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:47.760
fights between Kevin’s parents eventually&nbsp;
resulting in a divorce. To make things worse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:52.320
Kevin’s father died soon after the divorce&nbsp;
when Kevin was just 7 years old. This forced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.240
Kevin’s mother, Georgette, to really grind on the&nbsp;
business and look for other streams of income.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:01.760
Most people in this situation would generally&nbsp;
pick up a second job and/or downscale their lives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:06.240
but interestingly enough, Georgette decided&nbsp;
to make up the discrepancy through investing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:10.720
Every week, Georgette invested ⅓ of her&nbsp;
paycheck in large-cap dividend-paying stocks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:15.920
and this is what really sparked Kevin’s interest&nbsp;
in business and investing. Fortunately, things got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:20.720
better financially after Georgette married an&nbsp;
economist named George Kanawaty. Given that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:25.440
worked for United Nations though, the family had&nbsp;
to constantly move around the world. Usually, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.880
would be a disadvantage because moving is often&nbsp;
just a nuisance. But, in Kevin’s case, I think it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:35.520
was actually a massive advantage because he wasn’t&nbsp;
moving from Minnesota to Michigan or something.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.560
He was moving from Cyprus to Cambodia to&nbsp;
Ethiopia to Tunisia. Each of these places&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.960
had radically different cultures, languages,&nbsp;
and traditions which really opened Kevin’s eyes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:49.200
to the other side of the world. This actually&nbsp;
inspired Kevin to capture his experiences by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:54.320
becoming a photographer, but due to his stepfather&nbsp;
insisting on him attending college, Kevin would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:58.800
end up at the University of Waterloo where he&nbsp;
majored in environmental studies and psychology.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.680
He would quickly turn around a complete an MBA&nbsp;
at the University of Western Ontario in 1980,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.800
but this was basically the end of&nbsp;
Kevin’s journey through the normal path.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:10.560
SET ON BUSINESS:&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.360
Kevin was pretty set on avoiding a regular 9 to&nbsp;
5 ever since his first job at an ice cream shop.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:19.200
Kevin was hired as an ice cream scooper and while&nbsp;
this seemed like a pretty chill high school job to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.920
earn some extra money, the money isn’t what drew&nbsp;
Kevin to the shop. The main reason Kevin applied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:28.480
for the job is because the shop was located in&nbsp;
a mall, and his crush, Elizabeth, had a job at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.880
the store right across the ice cream shop. Kevin&nbsp;
was hoping that by working at the ice cream shop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:37.440
Elizabeth would notice him and they could&nbsp;
potentially grab lunch one day. But, Kevin’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:42.560
dreams were shattered on the second day. You see,&nbsp;
right as his first shift came to an end, Elizabeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:47.760
would notice him. This made Kevin ecstatic as he&nbsp;
could talk to her right after the job ended. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:52.320
then, the owner of the shop asked Kevin to scrape&nbsp;
the gum off the floor before leaving for the day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:57.520
Given that Elizabeth had noticed him, Kevin didn’t&nbsp;
want to be seen doing such a job, so he came up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:02.480
with a witty response saying that he was hired&nbsp;
to be a scooper, not a scraper. And as you would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:07.440
guess, this got Kevin fired. As soon as Kevin got&nbsp;
home, he complained to his stepfather about his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:12.720
experience and told him that he never wanted to&nbsp;
work another job. To this, his stepfather replied,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:16.640
well even if you’re self-employed, you’ll&nbsp;
be responsible to customers and shareholders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:21.200
who are often just as demanding if not more&nbsp;
demanding. Kevin says that he refused to believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:26.800
his stepfather at the time, but now, he realizes&nbsp;
that he was completely right. Nonetheless, Kevin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:31.360
was determined to avoid a traditional job, and&nbsp;
this is what drove him towards photography, but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:36.000
didn’t take him long to channel this energy into a&nbsp;
business. After college, he would end up starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:40.800
Special Event Television or SET with two of his&nbsp;
MBA classmates. They put out some semi-successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:45.040
sports programming including The Original Six,&nbsp;
Don Cherry’s Grapevine, and Bobby Orr and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:49.840
Hockey League. They also produced soccer films,&nbsp;
sports documentaries, and sports commercials.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:55.040
While this was somewhat successful, Kevin knew&nbsp;
that this wasn’t the path he wanted to go down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:00.160
so when one of his partners offered to buy his&nbsp;
stake for $25,000, Kevin would take the deal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:04.560
He instantly turned around and used this money to&nbsp;
start a new company and that brings us to Softkey.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:10.320
SOFTKEY:
Kevin founded Softkey in 1986 with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:15.200
two business partners named John Freeman and Gary&nbsp;
Babcock. Middle-class families were just starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:19.520
to get their first computers around this time, so&nbsp;
Kevin knew that there would be a lot of derived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:24.000
demand in the coming years. Looking back, I think&nbsp;
Kevin would’ve far preferred to start a software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:29.840
company like Adobe or Autodesk, but at the&nbsp;
time, he ended up choosing educational software.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:34.560
The reasoning for this was that the education&nbsp;
niche was under-targeted by competitors, but this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:39.840
wasn’t a mistake. You see, the education niche is&nbsp;
extremely difficult because either you’re dealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:44.320
with schools with extremely restricted budgets&nbsp;
and drawn-out approval processes or you’re dealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:48.720
with parents who don’t really see the value of&nbsp;
the product. The pitch to sell the product isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:53.360
particularly easy either because the benefit&nbsp;
is not monetary. With something like Photoshop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:58.080
the value is obvious. You get photoshop to produce&nbsp;
better pictures and drive in more revenue for your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:02.000
business. With educational software though,&nbsp;
parents have to hope that the software helps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:05.920
their kids get a better education, and hope that&nbsp;
it makes a meaningful difference in their lives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:10.080
and hope that it pays off when they grow up and&nbsp;
start making money. As you can see, there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:14.880
a lot of ifs and hopes and the ROI timeline is&nbsp;
extremely long which makes the business extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:19.280
difficult. It didn’t take long for Kevin to&nbsp;
realize this, and this forced him to place a heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:24.720
emphasis on marketing. Kevin says quote, “What’s&nbsp;
inside the box is important, but it’s not as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:29.280
important as how it’s marketed." Softkey was one&nbsp;
of the first companies to embrace the revolving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:33.840
CD rack which allowed them to display their CDs&nbsp;
more efficiently than any of their competitors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:38.480
Kevin also chose to display the product in more&nbsp;
general stores like Office Depot, Radio Shack, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:43.040
SmithBooks as opposed to just traditional software&nbsp;
stores. This showcased the product to a much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:47.840
broader audience leading to substantially more&nbsp;
revenue. Aside from smart marketing, Kevin also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:53.200
chose to keep the price of the software as low&nbsp;
as possible at $40 to $100. This left the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:57.680
with razor-thin profit margins, but allowed them&nbsp;
to move much more volume and made it difficult for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:01.840
new competitors to enter the scene. This allowed&nbsp;
Softkey to become the dominant education software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:06.240
company by the early 1990s, and with that&nbsp;
Kevin would start to buy out his competitors.

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:10.000
MAD CUSTOMER:&nbsp;

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:14.240
In 1993, SoftKey would merge with WorldStar&nbsp;
International and Spinnaker Software to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:18.560
SoftKey International. Just one year later,&nbsp;
they would purchase Software Marketing Corp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:22.320
using 600,000 shares of common stock,&nbsp;
but their most influential acquisition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:26.560
didn’t come till 1995 when they got into a&nbsp;
bidding war to purchase the Learning Company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:32.560
SoftKey did end up winning the war, but it would&nbsp;
cost them $606 million. Around the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:36.720
SoftKey would also purchase Minnesota Educational&nbsp;
Computing Corporation for $370 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:42.960
All of this made Softkey bigger than ever before,&nbsp;
but they were also more fragile than ever before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:47.520
All these acquisitions had taken a toll on&nbsp;
SoftKey’s balance sheet and their razor-thin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:52.640
margins didn’t help much either. Luckily though,&nbsp;
Mattel would offer to buy the company for $4.2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:57.760
billion in 1999. Kevin of course took the deal,&nbsp;
and this allowed him to sell his company at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:03.120
peak of the dot-com bubble. While this was a smart&nbsp;
move for Kevin, this was disastrous for Mattel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:07.920
In the year following the acquisition, Mattel’s&nbsp;
goal for SoftKey was a $50 million profit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:16.320
but the company ended up losing $105 million.&nbsp;
Investors were not happy at all by these results&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:20.480
and they would start to dump Mattel stock leading&nbsp;
to a $3 billion decrease in Mattel’s valuation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:25.040
in a single day. A group of Mattel’s investors&nbsp;
would get together and file a class-action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:30.320
lawsuit against Mattel and Kevin for misleading&nbsp;
investors and making a terrible acquisition. Kevin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:36.320
and Mattel would initially deny all charges, but&nbsp;
eventually, Mattel would give in. First, in 2000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:40.720
Mattel would decide to dissolve all of SoftKey’s&nbsp;
assets. They sold the education division to Gore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:45.360
Technology Group. They sold the entertainment&nbsp;
division to Ubisoft. And they sold the brand name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:50.160
of, The Learning Company, to Riverdeep Interactive&nbsp;
Learning. Mattel took massive losses on each of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:55.920
these sales, and to make things worse, they agreed&nbsp;
to settle the lawsuit in 2003 by paying out $122&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:01.360
million. This was truly quite unfortunate for&nbsp;
Mattel, but things couldn’t be better for Kevin.

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:05.280
FREEDOM:&nbsp;

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:10.000
Kevin says that he was never chasing money&nbsp;
with SoftKey but rather chasing freedom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:13.520
And now that he had sold his company, he&nbsp;
had more money than he could ever need.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:18.560
Kevin’s net worth is currently estimated to be&nbsp;
around $400 million, and though that’s a solid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:23.040
amount, it’s pretty modest compared to where he&nbsp;
could be. Even if Kevin just invested all of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:27.920
money in something like the S&amp;P 500, he’d likely&nbsp;
be worth a few billion dollars today, but Kevin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:32.960
has far preferred to preserve his wealth than grow&nbsp;
his wealth. He does have some speculative/risky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:37.920
investments in his portfolio like Bitcoin and&nbsp;
Tesla, but these account for a very small portion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:42.880
of his portfolio and he doesn’t let them grow&nbsp;
past 5% of his portfolio. Aside from limiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:46.960
his exposure to speculative investments, Kevin&nbsp;
likes to keep a lot of his wealth in cash and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:52.560
bonds which is obviously not the best for growth.&nbsp;
Kevin also avoids debt like the plague and advises&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:56.560
that people should get rid of their mortgage&nbsp;
debt as fast as possible. Considering this super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.560 --> 00:10:01.040
conservative investment style, it’s not clear if&nbsp;
Kevin will ever reach the billion-dollar mark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:05.920
but like I said, Kevin doesn’t really care about&nbsp;
that. It doesn’t really matter for his kids either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:10.560
as Kevin refuses to leave any inheritance money.&nbsp;
Kevin thinks that you curse a child when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:15.360
de-risk their lives and he absolutely despises&nbsp;
entitlement. He doesn’t want any of his children&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:19.680
or grandchildren to have a poor childhood though&nbsp;
due to bad parents. So, he created a trust that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:24.000
gives his descendants money up until the day&nbsp;
they graduate college. But after that, they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:29.120
on their own. In the meantime, Kevin has spent his&nbsp;
free time revisiting his passion for photography.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:34.000
Apparently, he owns a massive collection of Nikon&nbsp;
and Canon cameras and he owns every lens you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:38.400
think of. And considering all the work he put in&nbsp;
to get to this point, I think it’s only fair that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:44.800
he’s able to finally enjoy his wealth and explore&nbsp;
his passions. Are you guys a fan of Mr. Wonderful?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:49.200
Comment that down below. Also, drop a like if&nbsp;
you agree that Kevin is the most underrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:53.120
shark on Shark Tank. And of course, consider&nbsp;
joining our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

